.TH RUNCAPPED 1 "2026-06-12" "anomalous-mon" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
runcapped \- run a command inside a memory-capped systemd user scope
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B runcapped
.RB [ \-m
.IR MEM ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR SWAP ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I command
.RI [ args... ]
.br
.B runcapped
.RB { \-h | \-\-help }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B runcapped
launches
.I command
inside a transient systemd
.B \-\-user \-\-scope
cgroup with hard memory and swap ceilings
.RB ( MemoryMax ,
.BR MemoryHigh ,
.BR MemorySwapMax ).
If the command's memory use exceeds the cap, the kernel's cgroup OOM killer
terminates
.I only that command's cgroup
\(em not the rest of the desktop session.
.PP
This exists because an unconstrained workload that exhausts all RAM
.I and
all swap triggers the kernel's
.I global
OOM killer, which kills processes across the whole user session by score:
on 2026-06-12 such a job (a Python data script) took down dbus, gnome-keyring,
pipewire and Cinnamon, bouncing the user to the login screen. Capping the job
contains the blast radius. A small
.B MemorySwapMax
additionally makes a runaway
.I fail fast
instead of thrashing tens of GB of swap (and freezing the desktop) for minutes
before the OOM finally fires.
.PP
The command runs in the foreground attached to the terminal, so stdin/stdout,
Ctrl\-C, and the exit code behave exactly as if
.I command
had been run directly.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-m " MEM"
Hard memory cap, passed to systemd as
.BR MemoryMax " and " MemoryHigh .
Accepts systemd size suffixes (e.g.
.IR 18G ", " 512M ).
Default:
.BR 18G .
.TP
.BI \-s " SWAP"
Swap cap, passed as
.BR MemorySwapMax .
Default:
.BR 4G .
Use
.I 0
to forbid swap entirely (fail as soon as RAM is exhausted).
.TP
.BI \-n " NAME"
Scope unit name suffix. Defaults to the command's basename; for a bare
interpreter (python, bash, node, ...) the script name (next argument) is used
instead. Sanitized to
.BR [A\-Za\-z0\-9_\-] .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the embedded usage block and exit.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.B runcapped
.B exec()s
the command, so its exit status is the command's own. Argument errors (no
command given, bad option) exit
.BR 1 .
If the cgroup OOM killer terminates the command, the shell reports it as a
signal (typically
.BR SIGKILL ", 137).
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Run a memory-hungry data job under the default 18G/4G caps:
.PP
.RS
.EX
runcapped python scripts/run_sae_comparison.py --dataset asap_set1_2 \\
    --device cuda --save-matrices
.EE
.RE
.PP
Tighter cap, no swap, explicit name:
.PP
.RS
.EX
runcapped -m 8G -s 0 -n bigmatrix python train.py
.EE
.RE
.PP
Inspect a running capped job and watch its live memory:
.PP
.RS
.EX
systemctl --user status 'runcapped-*'
systemctl --user show -p MemoryCurrent 'runcapped-bigmatrix.scope'
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
.PP
Requires
.BR systemd\-run (1)
with user-session support (cgroup v2 with the memory controller delegated to
.BR user@.service ,
the default on modern systemd). There is no fallback: if
.BR systemd\-run
is unavailable the
.B exec
fails and the command does not run.
.PP
The cap protects the desktop from the job; it does
.I not
make the job itself succeed. A job that genuinely needs more than the cap will
be OOM-killed \(em raise
.B \-m
or reduce the job's footprint (for example, lower a parallel worker count that
copies large arrays per worker).
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR systemd\-run (1),
.BR systemd.resource\-control (5),
.BR anomalous\-mon (1)
.PP
Companion proactive monitor:
.B anomalous-mon
warns on memory pressure and per-process runaway growth
.I before
an OOM. See
.I PLAN-memory-monitor.md
and
.I FINDINGS-rclone-oom-2026-06-09.md
in the anomalous-mon repository.
